      "text": "To determine the total number of games played in the baseball league, we need to consider that each game involves two teams, and each team plays exactly 3 games against each of the other teams. Here is the step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. First, calculate the number of unique matchups between the nine teams. This is equivalent to finding the number of ways to choose 2 teams out of 9, which is given by the combination formula \\( \\binom{n}{2} \\), where \\( n \\) is the number of teams.\n   \\[\n   \\binom{9}{2} = \\frac{9 \\times 8}{2 \\times 1} = 36\n   \\]\n   So, there are 36 unique matchups between the teams.\n\n2. Since each matchup consists of 2 games (one game for each of the two teams), the total number of games played is half of the number of unique matchups.\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total number of games} = \\frac{36}{2} = 18\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the total number of games played in the league is \\(\\boxed{18}\\).",
